WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:02.101
-I think it's very true to say

00:00:02.101 --> 00:00:03.402
that show business
can be a very fake place.

00:00:03.402 --> 00:00:06.339
I do think that's one of
the realest nights insofar as

00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:08.507
you spend a lot of time
talking to people

00:00:08.507 --> 00:00:10.076
you're a genuine fan of,

00:00:10.076 --> 00:00:12.711
and it's a night
that allows you to express that.

00:00:12.711 --> 00:00:15.548
And I've had very cool
conversations with, you know,

00:00:15.548 --> 00:00:17.584
people I never
would have met otherwise.

00:00:17.584 --> 00:00:20.786
Where, they're in a show that
I, you know, genuinely love,

00:00:20.786 --> 00:00:22.255
and to go over
and be able to tell them

00:00:22.255 --> 00:00:24.390
is a really cool part
of the night.

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:26.392
-Sure. You know, when it's real,

00:00:26.392 --> 00:00:28.795
like you haven't seen someone
for a long time,

00:00:28.795 --> 00:00:30.463
and it's so great to see them

00:00:30.463 --> 00:00:32.298
and it's so great
to congratulate them.

00:00:32.298 --> 00:00:34.734
-You know, there's always
that clock of when you,

00:00:34.734 --> 00:00:37.403
not that I would know,
but when you win,

00:00:37.403 --> 00:00:39.139
there's a clock that
starts counting down.

00:00:39.139 --> 00:00:40.640
And so you know you only
have 30 seconds left.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:44.010
The other clock is anytime
you start talking to someone

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:46.045
you can't believe
you're talking to.

00:00:46.045 --> 00:00:48.981
It's like 15 seconds
until a weirdo interrupts.

00:00:48.981 --> 00:00:50.349
[ Laughs ]
-Correct.

00:00:50.349 --> 00:00:53.886
Or you feel -- you feel them
like right here,

00:00:53.886 --> 00:00:56.222
kind of like hovering,
hovering, right?

00:00:56.222 --> 00:00:59.859
Or else it's like -- and then
it's also, see, I do find things

00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:02.362
like award shows very
interesting, because, like,

00:01:02.362 --> 00:01:05.564
you get to see
one's own ego at play,

00:01:05.564 --> 00:01:09.335
and just, like, a social dynamic
that is crazy to see.

00:01:09.335 --> 00:01:13.005
Because you're constantly
moving in, like,

00:01:13.005 --> 00:01:15.674
high-low, high-low,
high-low, high-low.

00:01:15.674 --> 00:01:18.144
And then you have to
kind of stay firm

00:01:18.144 --> 00:01:20.179
to, like, what your position is.

00:01:20.179 --> 00:01:24.651
It's like, oh, someone else,
more -- oh, Lady Gaga's passing.

00:01:24.651 --> 00:01:26.185
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:26.185 --> 00:01:28.521
-Okay, so she's --
You know what I mean?

00:01:28.521 --> 00:01:32.024
So there's always...

00:01:32.024 --> 00:01:34.960
I don't want to use the word
as hard as hierarchy,

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:37.763
but there is a hierarchy
that is constantly moving,

00:01:37.763 --> 00:01:40.266
and you've got to kind of
figure out your play.

00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:43.536
So that's why -- that's why I've
actually seen people that I know

00:01:43.536 --> 00:01:46.071
that I like that I have some
sort of like relationship with.

00:01:46.071 --> 00:01:47.507
It's really, really
kind of great to see

00:01:47.507 --> 00:01:49.175
because it's really
super grounding.

00:01:49.175 --> 00:01:52.612
-It is. I think that's
a really nice way of putting it.

00:01:52.612 --> 00:01:55.748
You and I have something else --
we have very little in common

00:01:55.748 --> 00:01:58.350
because you're such an
accomplished actor and I'm not.

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:01.653
But we have both worked --
we both worked with

00:02:01.653 --> 00:02:03.622
the great Garry Marshall,

00:02:03.622 --> 00:02:07.427
who was one of the, like,
warmest souls in the world.

00:02:07.427 --> 00:02:08.561
-Yes.

00:02:08.561 --> 00:02:12.665
-And your work in
"The Princess Diaries"

00:02:12.665 --> 00:02:15.100
has come back around recently.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:18.370
You have a wonderfully delivered
line in the film

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:20.306
about how the Queen
is coming to the school.

00:02:20.306 --> 00:02:23.042
And I know you talked about this
recently on "Graham Norton,"

00:02:23.042 --> 00:02:27.346
but now people are lip-syncing
to your scene on TikTok in a way

00:02:27.346 --> 00:02:29.615
that the scene did not inform
how people

00:02:29.615 --> 00:02:31.951
would choose to do this.

00:02:31.951 --> 00:02:36.389
-Someone sent that to me,
and I could not believe it.

00:02:36.389 --> 00:02:39.125
And it's this
great kind of TikTok --

00:02:39.125 --> 00:02:40.659
I don't even know
what to call it.

00:02:40.659 --> 00:02:44.263
This TikTok string, chain.

00:02:44.263 --> 00:02:46.466
I didn't even know what it is,
but it's on TikTok,

00:02:46.466 --> 00:02:49.668
and it takes one line
that Vice Principal Gupta,

00:02:49.668 --> 00:02:52.805
who I played in the film,

00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:55.841
and people seem to be
just taking that one line,

00:02:55.841 --> 00:02:58.544
because my character is
talking on the phone.

00:02:58.544 --> 00:03:01.848
And instead of the phone,
they use something else.

00:03:01.848 --> 00:03:04.684
-Yeah.
-And it's freaking hilarious.

00:03:04.684 --> 00:03:06.852
And it's actually
a really good line

00:03:06.852 --> 00:03:08.554
to do something like that with.

00:03:08.554 --> 00:03:11.257
-It's a really good line.
People are using --

00:03:11.257 --> 00:03:14.494
We'll show a clip of one,
but people are using their pets.

00:03:14.494 --> 00:03:15.461
-Yes.

00:03:15.461 --> 00:03:17.763
[ Telephone ringing ]
-Gupta.

00:03:17.763 --> 00:03:23.636
Mm-hmm, mm-hmm, mm-hmm.

00:03:23.636 --> 00:03:24.804
The Queen is coming.

00:03:24.804 --> 00:03:26.472
-I went back
and re-watched the line.

00:03:26.472 --> 00:03:30.576
It is, I hope you know --
It's so perfectly delivered.

00:03:30.576 --> 00:03:31.811
I mean, do you ever look back?

00:03:31.811 --> 00:03:33.312
Now that it's like...
-No.

00:03:33.312 --> 00:03:36.148
-...becoming this viral moment,
do you just think like,

00:03:36.148 --> 00:03:38.350
"Thank God I nailed that line
with the precision --"

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:39.852
-You know what? No.

00:03:39.852 --> 00:03:42.188
And going back to just a little
bit about Garry Marshall,

00:03:42.188 --> 00:03:48.361
I remember the main and only
direction he gave to me --

00:03:48.361 --> 00:03:49.662
this might be familiar to you.

00:03:49.662 --> 00:03:52.297
He would go,
"Come here. Come here.

00:03:52.297 --> 00:03:55.334
Go out and make funny, huh?
Go out and make funny."

00:03:55.334 --> 00:03:57.103
And I will always remember it

00:03:57.103 --> 00:03:58.804
because he gave me
a lot of freedom.

00:03:58.804 --> 00:04:01.140
-Yeah.
-He gave me a lot of freedom

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:03.543
to try and be
as funny as possible.

00:04:03.543 --> 00:04:05.478
But I've also --

00:04:05.478 --> 00:04:08.281
So, yeah. I actually,
honestly, have not seen it.

00:04:08.281 --> 00:04:11.050
I haven't seen that
in a long time.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:13.986
But I really enjoy
how people enjoy it.

00:04:13.986 --> 00:04:16.789
And people, I will say,
in the past --

00:04:16.789 --> 00:04:18.290
now it's something
totally different --

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:23.696
but in the past, young people
or young people, like,

00:04:23.696 --> 00:04:25.698
who are in their 20s
at that point,

00:04:25.698 --> 00:04:29.569
would come up to me
and just say, "Oh, my God."

00:04:29.569 --> 00:04:30.870
And they would do the line.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:32.705
So I'm like, "Oh, my goodness.

00:04:32.705 --> 00:04:36.475
Did this line hit for, like,
you know, 8-year-olds,

00:04:36.475 --> 00:04:39.145
and now they're grown or maybe
now they're the people

00:04:39.145 --> 00:04:40.946
who are like, you know,
wanting to --

00:04:40.946 --> 00:04:44.149
-Yeah, it's really amazing
to think that you're doing

00:04:44.149 --> 00:04:47.253
a piece of work, you know,
for the purpose at hand then,

00:04:47.253 --> 00:04:49.255
but you're also planting
this seed

00:04:49.255 --> 00:04:51.290
that you're completely
unaware of,

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:55.194
because of however it was that
they, you know, took it in

00:04:55.194 --> 00:04:57.697
the first time they saw it,
and now it's just like

00:04:57.697 --> 00:05:00.933
planting this wonderful new life
for this one line in a movie.

00:05:00.933 --> 00:05:03.002
-Yes, it's a full story.

00:05:03.002 --> 00:05:05.738
I think that's also why it is,
is in that line,

00:05:05.738 --> 00:05:08.307
is that you have a full story.

00:05:08.307 --> 00:05:13.746
It's a good comedic-written
scene, and I guess you're right.

00:05:13.746 --> 00:05:16.115
It's like -- because you just
have these different beats

00:05:16.115 --> 00:05:19.885
of how can you do it, and even
if you just look directionally,

00:05:19.885 --> 00:05:23.722
with the beats,
you can make some of them fun.

00:05:23.722 --> 00:05:26.191
-Well, I hope retroactively --

00:05:26.191 --> 00:05:28.194
I'm very happy for
your Emmy nomination.

00:05:28.194 --> 00:05:30.629
I hope retroactively you will
get an Oscar nomination

00:05:30.629 --> 00:05:33.232
for that role if they ever
start doing that.

00:05:33.232 --> 00:05:36.235
And thank you. It's always
so great to see you, Sandra.

00:05:36.235 --> 00:05:38.404
-Same, same, what a pleasure.

